// Reference query. Not deployed in any employer environment.
// Title: One Microsoft Entra sign-in session seen from more than one country, or from a new network and a new client, within a short window (possible stolen session replay)
// Status: untested template
// ATT&CK: T1539 (Steal Web Session Cookie), T1550.004 (Use Alternate Authentication Material: Web Session Cookie); ATT&CK v19.2
// Data sources: Microsoft Defender XDR advanced hunting, EntraIdSignInEvents (preview; Microsoft documents that it replaces AADSignInEventsBeta on 2026-10-19)
// Licensing assumptions: Microsoft documents a Microsoft Entra ID P2 requirement for this table (scenario holds Entra ID P2 through Microsoft 365 E5); the risk event types used below are premium Entra ID Protection detections
// Rule ID: DX-03 | Platform: Defender XDR advanced hunting, custom detection candidate | Suggested severity: High for a country change, Medium otherwise
// Tactics: Credential Access (TA0006), Lateral Movement (TA0008)
// Answers: detection-priorities.md item 5 (Priority 1); attack-paths.md AP-1 steps 5 and 6
// Architecture context: PE-IDENTITY, PA-IDENTITY, PEP-SESSION-PROXY (CA-10), PIP-ID-RISK; residual risk RR-03; risk register R-01
// Fictional scenario: Contoso Regional Health (fictional). Reference portfolio content.
//
// Purpose
//   Compensate for the acknowledged token-theft gap (RR-03): the EHR browser path and guest or unmanaged
//   sessions are not enforced by continuous access evaluation, so a session captured by an
//   adversary-in-the-middle page stays usable until it expires. Detection is the compensating control.
//   This rule watches the replay of the session (AP-1 steps 5 and 6). The capture itself (step 4,
//   T1557) is left to Entra ID Protection's own detection (Logic step 5).
//
// Logic (a heuristic designed for this pack, not a Microsoft-published detection)
//   1. Successful sign-ins (ErrorCode 0) with a SessionId, from every address, Contoso egress included,
//      so the legitimate half of a replayed session stays in the group.
//   2. Grouped per account and SessionId in fixed time bins (ReplayWindow).
//   3. A group is returned only when at least one of its sign-ins comes from outside TrustedEgressIPs.
//      Strong signal: the same session from two or more countries in one bin.
//      Medium signal: the same session from two or more IP addresses with two or more user agents.
//   4. Boost when Entra ID Protection already tagged a sign-in in the group with anomalousToken,
//      tokenIssuerAnomaly, or unfamiliarFeatures, which Microsoft's risk detection reference lists as
//      sign-in risk detections. The reference lists unfamiliarFeatures as real-time, anomalousToken as
//      real-time or offline, and tokenIssuerAnomaly as offline. An offline detection may be absent from
//      the sign-in row when the row is ingested, and the boost then does not apply.
//   5. Attacker in the Middle (attackerinTheMiddle) is not used. Microsoft's reference lists it only as a
//      user risk detection, calculated offline, and documents no path for it into a sign-in row's
//      RiskEventTypes. T1557 is credited to that native detection, not to this rule (coverage-map.md
//      section 2).
//   Interactive and non-interactive sign-ins are not split here. Microsoft's table reference describes
//   LogonType with Windows logon types (interactive, remote interactive, network, batch, and service) that
//   do not match Entra's interactive and non-interactive split, so the query does not use that column. The
//   Sentinel tables SigninLogs and AADNonInteractiveUserSignInLogs allow the split if a later revision
//   needs it.
//
// Known false positives
//   - Mobile clinicians moving between hospital Wi-Fi and cellular networks (IP and sometimes country of
//     the carrier egress change). Country changes near borders or through carrier roaming.
//   - Consumer VPNs on personal devices used by affiliated physicians.
//   - Traffic through Global Secure Access, where the observed address can be the service egress; the
//     output counts those sign-ins (ThroughGSA) so an analyst can discount them.
//
// Tuning knobs (let statements below)
//   ReplayWindow        bin size; shorter is more precise, longer catches slower replay.
//   TrustedEgressIPs    Contoso egress and known provider addresses (documentation addresses shown). A
//                       group needs at least one sign-in from outside them. Sign-ins from them still count
//                       toward countries, addresses, and clients.
//   RiskSignals         Entra ID Protection sign-in riskEventType values that raise confidence.
//
// Response notes
//   Revoke the user's sessions and refresh tokens in Entra ID (PA-IDENTITY), terminate EHR browser sessions
//   through the EHR administrative function (A-07, CAE does not reach the EHR), and follow the identity
//   compromise playbook. Revocation of a clinician's session needs the clinical notification step (ADR-008).
//
// Validation notes
//   Not run against any tenant. Validate in a lab tenant with a test account: sign in, then present the same
//   session from a second network and client. Expected telemetry: two successful EntraIdSignInEvents rows
//   with the same SessionId and different IPAddress values. Run it once with the first sign-in from an
//   address listed in TrustedEgressIPs, and confirm that the group is still returned (Logic step 3). Record
//   whether RiskEventTypes carries any RiskSignals value on those rows. Do not use real staff accounts.
//   Microsoft Defender for Office 365 Attack simulation training covers the phishing delivery step for
//   consenting test users (purple-team-plan.md exercises PX-07 and PX-08). PX-08 validates the session
//   replay (T1539, T1550.004). T1557 is outside this rule (Logic step 5) and is not exercised.
//
// References
//   https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1539/
//   https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1550/004/
//   https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/defender-xdr/advanced-hunting-entraidsigninevents-table
//   https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/id-protection/concept-identity-protection-risks
//   https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/security/operations/token-theft-playbook
//
let LookbackWindow = 1d;
let ReplayWindow = 2h;
// Contoso egress and approved provider addresses (RFC 5737 documentation addresses shown as placeholders)
let TrustedEgressIPs = dynamic(["203.0.113.10", "203.0.113.11"]);
// Sign-in risk detections only; attackerinTheMiddle is a user risk detection (Logic step 5)
let RiskSignals = dynamic(["anomalousToken", "tokenIssuerAnomaly", "unfamiliarFeatures"]);
EntraIdSignInEvents
| where Timestamp > ago(LookbackWindow)
| where ErrorCode == 0
| where isnotempty(SessionId) and isnotempty(IPAddress)
// Contoso egress rows stay in the group (Logic step 1); empty Country values are not counted as a country
| summarize arg_max(Timestamp, ReportId, AccountUpn, AccountDisplayName, Application, ResourceDisplayName, IsManaged, IsCompliant, IsGuestUser),
    FirstSeen = min(Timestamp),
    DistinctCountries = dcountif(Country, isnotempty(Country)),
    Countries = make_set_if(Country, isnotempty(Country), 10),
    DistinctIPs = dcount(IPAddress),
    IPs = make_set(IPAddress, 10),
    DistinctUserAgents = dcount(UserAgent),
    UserAgents = make_set(UserAgent, 5),
    SignInsOutsideTrustedEgress = countif(IPAddress !in (TrustedEgressIPs)),
    RiskTaggedSignIns = countif(RiskEventTypes has_any (RiskSignals)),
    ThroughGSA = countif(IsSignInThroughGlobalSecureAccess)
    by AccountObjectId, SessionId, TimeBin = bin(Timestamp, ReplayWindow)
| where SignInsOutsideTrustedEgress > 0
| where DistinctCountries > 1 or (DistinctIPs > 1 and DistinctUserAgents > 1)
| extend SuggestedSeverity = case(
    DistinctCountries > 1 and RiskTaggedSignIns > 0, "High: session used from more than one country and tagged by Entra ID Protection",
    DistinctCountries > 1, "High: session used from more than one country",
    RiskTaggedSignIns > 0, "Medium-high: new network and client for the session, tagged by Entra ID Protection",
    "Medium: new network and client for the same session")
| extend UnmanagedDevice = IsManaged == 0
| project Timestamp, ReportId, AccountObjectId, AccountUpn, AccountDisplayName, SuggestedSeverity, SessionId, Countries, IPs, UserAgents,
    SignInsOutsideTrustedEgress, RiskTaggedSignIns, ThroughGSA, Application, ResourceDisplayName, UnmanagedDevice, IsCompliant, IsGuestUser,
    FirstSeen, TimeBin
